---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyArea from '@/components/tools/everyday/CalcMoneyArea.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="tile-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyArea client:load variant="tile" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the tile calculator counts tiles</h2>
    <p>
      The area is the surface to cover; the tile count is what you buy. The grout joint and the waste allowance
      make the difference between them.
    </p>
    <pre><code>effective tile area = (width + joint) × (height + joint) ÷ 144   [sq ft]
tiles needed        = area × (1 + waste) ÷ effective tile area, rounded up
boxes               = tiles ÷ tiles per box, rounded up</code></pre>
    <p>
      A 12 × 12 inch tile with a 1/8 inch joint covers 12.125 × 12.125 inches, or 1.0209 square feet. So a
      hundred square feet needs 98 tiles before waste, not 100, and 108 with a 10% allowance.
    </p>

    <h2>Grout joints matter most on small tiles</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[460px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Tile size</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Joint</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Area taken by grout</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Tiles per 100 sq ft</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>2 × 2 in mosaic</td><td style="text-align:right">1/8"</td><td style="text-align:right">11.4%</td><td style="text-align:right">3,189</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4 × 4 in</td><td style="text-align:right">1/8"</td><td style="text-align:right">6.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">846</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6 × 6 in</td><td style="text-align:right">1/8"</td><td style="text-align:right">4.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">384</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12 × 12 in</td><td style="text-align:right">1/8"</td><td style="text-align:right">2.1%</td><td style="text-align:right">98</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12 × 24 in</td><td style="text-align:right">1/16"</td><td style="text-align:right">0.8%</td><td style="text-align:right">50</td></tr>
          <tr><td>24 × 24 in</td><td style="text-align:right">1/16"</td><td style="text-align:right">0.5%</td><td style="text-align:right">25</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm">
      Common joints as decimals: 1/16" = 0.0625, 1/8" = 0.125, 3/16" = 0.1875, 1/4" = 0.25.
    </p>
    <p>
      On a large-format floor the joint makes almost no difference. On mosaic it takes more than a tenth of
      the coverage, which is enough to leave you two sheets short.
    </p>

    <h2>How much waste to allow</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>10%: straight grid layout, simple rectangular room.</li>
      <li>15%: diagonal, herringbone or brick-bond layouts, which need many more cuts.</li>
      <li>15–20%: rooms with many angles, niches, alcoves or curved edges.</li>
      <li>A few spares: in addition to the percentage, keep whole tiles for future repairs.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The waste figure is off-cuts you cannot reuse, tiles that break when you cut them, and the pieces at the
      room edge where less than half a tile is left.
    </p>

    <h2>Buy from one batch</h2>
    <p>
      Ceramic and porcelain tiles are made in batches, and colour and size vary measurably between batches.
      For this reason most manufacturers print a batch or "shade" code on the box. A second box ordered a
      month later often makes a visible band across the floor. Buy the whole quantity, including waste, in one
      order, and keep the leftovers.
    </p>

    <h2>Floors and walls in one job</h2>
    <p>
      Add a row for each surface. Use the rectangle shape for a floor and the wall shape for vertical areas,
      and set a quantity for identical walls: three shower walls at 3 × 7 ft is one row with a quantity of
      three. The totals cover the whole job, so you take one box count to the merchant.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no negative-area row: measure the tiled surface directly. A shower wall with a window is easier
      to enter as two rectangles than as one rectangle minus a hole.
    </p>

    <h2>The other materials</h2>
    <p>
      This calculator counts tiles. The other materials scale with the same area, approximately:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Adhesive: about 4–6 kg per m² (about 1 lb per sq ft) with a notched trowel on a flat floor, and more
        for large-format tiles that need back-buttering.
      </li>
      <li>
        Grout: depends on tile size, joint width and depth. Manufacturers publish a coverage chart; small
        tiles with wide joints use several times more than large-format tiles.
      </li>
      <li>
        Spacers, trim and edging: counted by the perimeter, not the area.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Metric tiles</h2>
    <p>
      European tiles are sold in millimetres; 300 × 300, 600 × 300 and 600 × 600 are the common sizes. Convert
      to inches (divide by 25.4) for the tile dimension fields, or measure the room in metres and read the
      square-metre total, which European merchants quote against. 300 mm is 11.81 in; 600 mm is 23.62 in.
    </p>

    <h2>Works offline</h2>
    <p>
      The calculator runs in your browser and makes no network request, so it works in a half-renovated
      bathroom with no signal. Your measurement list is stored only if you turn saving on, and only on this
      device.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
